        content: "Argumentative writing aims to persuade the reader to accept a position or viewpoint. Writers build arguments using a combination of logic, evidence, and rhetorical devices.\n\nKey features to identify:\n- Thesis statement: The writer's central claim, usually stated early.\n- Evidence and support: Facts, statistics, expert opinions, examples, anecdotes used to back up claims.\n- Logical connectors: \"Therefore\", \"consequently\", \"however\", \"on the other hand\", \"furthermore\" - these signal how ideas relate.\n- Counterarguments: Strong arguments acknowledge opposing views and refute them. Look for \"Some might argue...\" or \"Critics suggest...\"\n- Tone: Often assertive and confident. May shift between measured and passionate depending on the point.\n\nHow to analyse in the exam:\n- Identify the writer's central argument first.\n- Note how they structure their reasoning (strongest point first? Building to climax?).\n- Look for rhetorical devices: rhetorical questions, repetition, rule of three.\n- Assess whether the argument is balanced or one-sided.\n- Comment on the effectiveness of evidence used.",
