        content: "Persuasive language goes beyond logical argument - it appeals to emotions, values, and identity. Persuasion works by making the audience FEEL something, not just think something.\n\nThe three classical appeals (Aristotle):\n- Ethos: Appeal to credibility. The writer establishes authority or trustworthiness. E.g., citing experience, credentials, or shared values.\n- Pathos: Appeal to emotion. Uses vivid imagery, personal stories, emotive diction to provoke feelings like sympathy, anger, hope, or fear.\n- Logos: Appeal to logic. Uses facts, statistics, cause-and-effect reasoning, and structured arguments.\n\nPersuasive techniques to identify:\n- Emotive language: Words chosen to provoke emotional response (\"heartbreaking\", \"devastating\", \"inspiring\").\n- Inclusive pronouns: \"We\", \"our\", \"us\" - creates a sense of shared identity and community.\n- Direct address: \"You\" - makes the reader feel personally involved.\n- Anaphora: Repetition at the start of successive sentences for emphasis and rhythm.\n- Rhetorical questions: Questions asked for effect, not answers. \"Can we really stand by and do nothing?\"\n- Rule of three: Grouping ideas in threes for impact. \"We came, we saw, we conquered.\"\n- Hyperbole: Deliberate exaggeration to emphasise a point.\n- Contrast/antithesis: Placing opposing ideas together. \"Ask not what your country can do for you, ask what you can do for your country.\"",
