        title: "Personal Essay",
        content: "The personal essay is a reflective piece where you explore your own thoughts, experiences, and feelings on a topic. It's one of the most popular composition choices because it allows genuine personal voice.\n\nStructure:\n- Introduction: Hook the reader with an anecdote, question, or striking statement. Introduce the topic and your angle.\n- Body (3-4 paragraphs): Each paragraph explores a different aspect, experience, or reflection. Move from specific memories to broader insights.\n- Conclusion: Reflect on what you've learned or how you've changed. Leave the reader with a thought-provoking final line.\n\nKey characteristics:\n- Authentic voice: Write as yourself. Let your personality come through.\n- Specific detail: Concrete memories and moments, not vague generalisations. \"I remember the smell of rain on hot tarmac\" beats \"I remember that day.\"\n- Reflection: Don't just describe - analyse. What did the experience MEAN? How did it change you?\n- Vulnerability: The best personal essays show honesty about doubts, mistakes, and growth.\n- Varied tone: Mix humour with seriousness, nostalgia with insight.\n\nCommon topics: disappointments, turning points, people who influenced you, lessons learned, identity, belonging, growing up.\n\nPCLM breakdown:\n- Purpose (30): Clear engagement with the topic, sustained personal voice.\n- Coherence (30): Logical flow, good paragraphing, clear structure.\n- Language (30): Varied vocabulary, imagery, sentence variety, appropriate tone.\n- Mechanics (10): Spelling, grammar, punctuation accuracy.",
