        content: "Cultural Context examines the world of the text - the society, values, norms, and historical setting that shape characters and events.\n\nWhat to look for:\n- Social structures: Class system, hierarchy, who holds power in society.\n- Gender roles: How are men and women expected to behave? Who conforms? Who rebels?\n- Economic conditions: Wealth, poverty, work, class mobility.\n- Religious/moral values: What beliefs guide the society? How are they enforced?\n- Historical period: When is the text set? What was happening in that era?\n- Family structures: Marriage, parenthood, family dynamics, inheritance.\n- Education and knowledge: Who has access to education? What role does it play?\n- Law and justice: How does the legal system work? Is it fair?\n- Community and belonging: Who belongs? Who is excluded? Why?\n\nComparing Cultural Contexts:\n- Look for similarities: Both texts may show patriarchal societies, or rigid class systems, or the consequences of conformity.\n- Look for differences: One text may show a community that protects its members while another shows one that destroys them.\n- Consider how cultural context SHAPES characters: Does the society enable or restrict them?\n- Consider how characters RESPOND to their context: Do they conform, rebel, escape, or adapt?\n\nPart (a) - 30 marks: Discuss ONE text in depth. Show how cultural context influences a central character's behaviour.\nPart (b) - 40 marks: COMPARE how cultural context influences characters in TWO other texts. Must use comparison language throughout.",
