        title: "Language Features in Poetry",
        content: "Recognising and discussing language features is the core skill for unseen poetry analysis. Here are the key features with examples of how to discuss them.\n\nImagery:\n- Visual imagery: Creates pictures in the reader's mind. \"The sun bled across the horizon.\"\n- Auditory imagery: Creates sound. \"The clock ticked its relentless rhythm.\"\n- Tactile imagery: Creates sensation of touch. \"The rough bark scraped her palms.\"\n- Olfactory/gustatory: Smell and taste. Less common but powerful when used.\n\nFigurative language:\n- Metaphor: Direct comparison. \"Time is a thief.\" Identifies one thing AS another.\n- Extended metaphor: A metaphor developed across several lines or the whole poem.\n- Simile: Comparison using like/as. \"Her voice was like honey.\"\n- Personification: Giving human qualities to non-human things. \"The wind whispered.\"\n- Symbolism: An object representing a larger concept. A rose = love, a road = life journey.\n\nSound devices:\n- Alliteration: Repeated initial consonants. Creates rhythm, emphasis, or mood.\n- Assonance: Repeated vowel sounds. Creates musicality or mood.\n- Consonance: Repeated consonant sounds within or at end of words.\n- Onomatopoeia: Words that sound like what they describe (buzz, crash, murmur).\n- Rhyme: End rhyme, internal rhyme, half-rhyme, slant rhyme.\n\nStructural devices:\n- Enjambment: Line runs on to next without punctuation. Creates flow, urgency, or continuation.\n- Caesura: Pause mid-line (usually punctuation). Creates emphasis, reflection, or dramatic effect.\n- Volta: A \"turn\" in the poem where meaning or tone shifts. Often at a stanza break.\n- Stanza structure: Regular stanzas suggest order; irregular suggests chaos or freedom.\n- Line length: Short lines = emphasis, urgency. Long lines = flow, expansion.",
