        title: "Figures of Speech",
        content: "Figures of speech are expressions that use words in non-literal ways to create effects. They are the building blocks of literary analysis.\n\nMetaphor: Describes something by saying it IS something else. \"Life is a journey.\" \"Time is a thief.\" Creates direct, powerful comparison. Extended metaphor = a metaphor developed across several lines or an entire text.\n\nSimile: Comparison using 'like' or 'as'. \"Her eyes sparkled like diamonds.\" \"He ran as fast as the wind.\" More explicit than metaphor.\n\nPersonification: Giving human qualities to non-human things. \"The wind howled.\" \"Justice is blind.\" Makes abstract concepts tangible.\n\nHyperbole: Deliberate exaggeration for emphasis. \"I've told you a million times.\" \"This bag weighs a ton.\" Not meant literally - used for effect.\n\nUnderstatement (Litotes): Deliberately representing something as less than it is. \"It's just a scratch\" (about a serious wound). Often ironic.\n\nOxymoron: Two contradictory words together. \"Bittersweet.\" \"Deafening silence.\" \"Living dead.\" Highlights paradox or complexity.\n\nParadox: A statement that seems contradictory but reveals a deeper truth. \"The only way to find yourself is to lose yourself.\"\n\nIrony: Gap between appearance and reality. Verbal irony = saying the opposite of what you mean. Dramatic irony = audience knows something characters don't. Situational irony = outcome is opposite to expectation.\n\nAllusion: A reference to another text, event, person, or cultural element. \"He was a real Romeo\" (allusion to Shakespeare). Assumes reader knowledge.\n\nEuphemism: A mild expression substituted for a harsh one. \"Passed away\" instead of \"died.\" \"Let go\" instead of \"fired.\"",
        keyTerms: ["metaphor", "simile", "personification", "hyperbole", "litotes", "oxymoron", "paradox", "irony", "allusion", "euphemism"],
        formula: null,
