        title: "Narrative and Dramatic Terms",
        content: "These terms are essential for discussing prose fiction (novels, short stories) and drama (plays).\n\nNarrative terms:\n- Narrator: The voice telling the story. Not always the author.\n- Point of view (POV): The perspective from which the story is told (first person, third person limited, third person omniscient, second person).\n- Unreliable narrator: A narrator whose account cannot be fully trusted (due to bias, limited knowledge, or deception).\n- Stream of consciousness: Narrative technique presenting thoughts as they occur, without conventional structure.\n- Interior monologue: Extended representation of a character's thoughts.\n- Free indirect discourse: Blending narrator's voice with character's thoughts without quotation marks.\n- Exposition: Background information provided to the reader.\n- Rising action: Events building toward the climax.\n- Climax: Moment of greatest tension or dramatic intensity.\n- Falling action: Events after the climax leading to resolution.\n- Denouement: The final unraveling or resolution of the plot.\n- Subplot: A secondary storyline that parallels or contrasts with the main plot.\n\nDramatic terms:\n- Soliloquy: A character speaks their thoughts aloud, alone on stage. Reveals inner conflict.\n- Aside: A brief remark to the audience, unheard by other characters.\n- Dramatic irony: The audience knows something a character does not.\n- Tragic flaw (hamartia): The character weakness that leads to the hero's downfall.\n- Catharsis: The emotional release experienced by the audience at the end of a tragedy.\n- Chorus: In Greek/some modern drama, a group that comments on the action.\n- Staging/mise-en-scene: The visual arrangement of a scene.\n- Monologue: An extended speech by one character to other characters present.",
