        content: "Spending 5-10 minutes planning before you write is the single most impactful thing you can do to improve your grades. A planned essay is always better than an unplanned one.\n\nStep 1 - Read and decode the question (2 min):\n- Underline key words and instruction words.\n- What EXACTLY is being asked?\n- What type of response is required (discuss, compare, analyse, to what extent)?\n\nStep 2 - Brainstorm (3 min):\n- Jot down every relevant idea, quote, example, technique that comes to mind.\n- Don't filter yet - get everything down.\n- For comparative: brainstorm for EACH text.\n\nStep 3 - Select and organise (3 min):\n- Choose your 3-5 strongest points.\n- Arrange them in logical order (chronological? thematic? ascending importance?).\n- For each point, note the evidence you'll use (quote or reference).\n- Decide your opening hook and closing thought.\n\nStep 4 - Write a brief outline:\n- Intro: Hook + thesis/position\n- Para 1: [Point] - [Evidence]\n- Para 2: [Point] - [Evidence]\n- Para 3: [Point] - [Evidence]\n- Para 4: [Point] - [Evidence]\n- Conclusion: [Synthesis + final thought]\n\nThis plan should fit in the margin of your answer booklet. It takes 5-10 minutes but saves you 15-20 minutes of indecision and restructuring while writing.\n\nPlanning prevents:\n- Going off-topic\n- Repeating yourself\n- Running out of things to say\n- Forgetting key quotes\n- Writing an unstructured response",
