        content: "Every body paragraph should follow a clear structure. This ensures your argument is developed, evidenced, and linked to the question.\n\nThe PEAL structure (works for all Paper 2 and most Paper 1 answers):\n\nP - Point: Start with a clear topic sentence that states the paragraph's main idea and links to the question.\nExample: \"Shakespeare uses the Fool as a dramatic device to expose Lear's failures as both king and father.\"\n\nE - Evidence: Provide specific textual evidence - a quote, reference to a scene, or specific detail.\nExample: \"The Fool tells Lear, 'Thou shouldst not have been old till thou hadst been wise,' directly criticising the king's decision to divide his kingdom.\"\n\nA - Analysis: Explain what the evidence shows. Discuss technique, language, effect. This is where you show your understanding.\nExample: \"The Fool's use of direct address and the conditional 'shouldst' implies that wisdom was within Lear's reach but he chose poorly. The inversion of the expected relationship - a fool instructing a king - creates dramatic irony and emphasises how far Lear has fallen.\"\n\nL - Link: Connect back to the question and/or transition to the next point.\nExample: \"This demonstrates Shakespeare's exploration of truth-telling, showing that in Lear's corrupted court, only the 'fool' dares speak honestly - a powerful commentary on how power silences truth.\"\n\nEach paragraph should be 8-12 sentences. Too short = underdeveloped. Too long = needs splitting.\n\nTransition between paragraphs:\n- \"Furthermore...\", \"Moreover...\", \"Building on this...\"\n- \"In contrast...\", \"However...\", \"On the other hand...\"\n- \"Similarly...\", \"This is also evident in...\"\n- \"Perhaps most significantly...\" (for your strongest point)",
